  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 1240 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nA heavy rainfall and significant flash flooding event is looking \nmore likely mid to late week across much of the area, especially the \neastern 2/3 of the area. The remnants of Polo will get caught up in \na in a deeper upper level trough that will develop across the \nSouthern Plains later this week. Also, expect abundant tropical \nmoisture connection from the Pacific(PW values 2+ inches) along \nwith strong lift with favorable upper level jet streak dynamics \nand a cold front in the area too. Looks like the main rounds of \nconvection will be Tuesday night through Thursday with medium \nchances of 2 inches or more of rainfall with low to medium chances\nof 4 to 6 inches mainly across the eastern half of the area. This\nwill result in possible significant flash flooding Tuesday night \nthrough Thursday. There is a slight to moderate risk of excessive \nrainfall across the area Tuesday through Thursday. Also, going \nwith medium- high chances of rain Wednesday afternoon and night. \nHave issued an SPS for the upcoming event. Keep up with the latest\nweather information. The rain chances will continue late this \nweek and the weekend with still a possible flood threat. The rain \nand clouds will finally cool things off, highs in the upper 70s \nand 80s.\n\nIn addition, there is a marginal to slight risk of severe weather\nWednesday across the area mainly during the afternoon and night. \nThe main hazards will be damaging winds and large hail along with \nlow chance tornado threat.",
